descriptions.NUR2936:
It is a corridor nuraghe with a vaguely quadrangular plan (18 x 15 m) and a singular protruding stretch of wall (length 7 m – width 3 m) at the south-west corner.

The wall face is of polygonal masonry with large, barely dressed blocks.

The monument is reduced to a heap of rubble that does not allow a clear reading of the internal spaces;

descriptions.NUR3200:
Corridor nuraghe of elliptical shape (17.90 x 11.85 m) surviving to a maximum height of 3 metres with 4–5 courses on the north-east side.

The masonry consists of medium and large blocks, roughly dressed, laid in rather irregular horizontal courses.

The monument has an entrance to the south (width 1.05 m – collapsed at the top) leading into the corridor, which cannot be walked through because it has collapsed but can be traced from above for about 5 metres;

descriptions.NUR2814:
The Nuraghe Cherbos is an extensive heap of rubble that in no way allows the ground plan of the Nuragic building to be defined; however, given the scale of the collapse it could turn out to be a multi-lobed nuraghe.

All around, for a considerable stretch, the remains of a large village of circular huts are evident.

Even in Taramelli's time the monument appeared in a very poor state of preservation: "destroyed: a heap of ruins 2 m high can be seen;
